Subject: [PATCH] default-on: fail ON, and stop the delay overflowing

Against Witty Pi 4 firmware V4.23 (d466cbc), WittyPi4.ino.

This firmware is full. Stock is 8190 of 8192 bytes — 99%, two bytes spare.
This patch uses both and lands at 8192 exactly, 100%. Nothing else will fit
without removing vendor code, which is why it does only the three things that
CANNOT be done anywhere else.

WHY ONLY THREE THINGS
---------------------
wittypi-configure.service runs `wittypi configure` on every boot and writes the
whole register policy, so any config register corrupted in EEPROM is
overwritten with the right value at the next boot. Configuration is already
self-healing in userspace.

That leaves exactly two registers userspace cannot rescue, because they act
inside setup() before Linux exists:

  17  DEFAULT_ON        decides whether the rail comes up at all
  47  DEFAULT_ON_DELAY  how long it waits, via an expression that overflows

Everything else belongs in /data/wittypi.env, and is there. The flash budget
and the correct division of labour happen to agree.

1. INVERT REGISTER 17
   Stock: `== 1`. One value of 256 powers the Pi on, 255 leave it waiting for a
   button press. On an unattended, hard-to-reach install that polarity is
   backwards, and the controller validates nothing: no CRC, no magic, no
   version, no range check anywhere in the image, and the EEPROM load adopts
   any byte that is not 255 verbatim.

   Now `!= 0x5A`: one value waits, 255 power on. Costs 0 bytes — same
   compare-and-branch.

   0x5A is chosen for DISTANCE, not convention. Retention decay drifts toward
   the erased 0xFF; an interrupted write of the 0x01 this register normally
   holds can land on 0x00. 0x5A is 5 bits from 0x01 and 4 from 0xFF.

   A wiped EEPROM now powers on by itself, because i2cReg zero-initialises
   and 0 != 0x5A. No seed needed — which matters, since the flash that installs
   this erases the EEPROM (hfuse 0xdd, EESAVE unprogrammed).

   Diverges from the vendor on one value: their documented "0 = no" now
   means ON. Use 90 to keep a board off.

2. MASK THE DEFAULT-ON DELAY AT THE POINT OF USE
   `delay(reg * 1000)` computes the product in a 16-bit int. Above 32 it wraps:
   254 reaches delay() as 4,294,959,152 ms — 49.7 days of rail-off,
   indistinguishable from dead hardware. A value like this has actually caused
   a real outage this way.

   `& 0x1F` bounds it to 31 (31000 fits int16) for 4 bytes. A compare-and-clamp
   on load cost 12 and did not fit. Masking at the point of use also guards the
   dangerous operation directly, with no branch to get wrong.

   The stored register is not modified, so a corrupt value above 32 still
   READS as itself while behaving as value & 31. Only reachable via corruption:
   the policy writes 10 and userspace refuses anything over 32.

   Not monotonic — 131 is worse than 254, and 229 is harmless. "Keep it
   small" cannot be applied by eye, which is why this is a mask and not advice.

3. SEED THE POWER-CUT DELAY AT 20.0 s
   Stock seeds 70 (7.0 s). The shutdown it must cover is 6 s measured plus an
   8 s mark-good grace = 14 s, so 7.0 s cuts the rail mid-write. Free: same
   instruction, different constant.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
---------------------------
An EEPROM-persistence fix (stop persisting live measurements and the
firmware revision, which otherwise get shadowed by stale EEPROM bytes) is
not included — it doesn't fit in the two spare bytes this firmware has.
Both motivations are survivable without it: wear only arms on a VIN
topology this node is not on, and the revision shadow is moot because
every flash erases the EEPROM anyway, so register 12 is re-seeded from
firmware regardless.

The site marker is dropped. Firmware is identified by reading flash back
and comparing against the built hex, which verifies the whole image rather than
one byte.

The RTC calibration offset is NOT seeded here. It is per-board — 119 is this
crystal — so a firmware seed would give another board a plausible WRONG
calibration rather than an obviously absent one. It lives in
/data/wittypi.env as WITTYPI_RTC_OFFSET, restored by configure at every boot.

BUILD AND VERIFY
----------------
  arduino-cli compile --fqbn ATTinyCore:avr:attinyx41:chip=841,clock=8internal,\
pinmapping=old,LTO=enable,wiremode=slave,neopixelport=porta,millis=disabled,\
eesave=aenable,bod=2v7,bodact=enabled,bodpd=enabled <sketch>

  stock   8190 bytes
  patched 8192 bytes  (+2, exactly the ceiling)

Do not verify by byte-comparing the hex. Adding two bytes shifts every
relative jump after it, so ~1300 bytes differ from code motion alone. Verify at
instruction level instead — each intended change is exactly +1 occurrence and
the value it replaces is exactly -1:

  cpi 0x5A   stock 0 -> patched 1     the sentinel
  ldi 0xC8   stock 7 -> patched 8     the 20.0 s seed
  andi 0x1F  stock 2 -> patched 3     the delay mask
  ldi 0x46   stock 1 -> patched 0     the vendor 7.0 s seed, gone

FLASHING
--------
FLASH ONLY. Never pass -U lfuse/hfuse/efuse. ISP is silicon rather than a
bootloader, so a failed or partial flash write leaves a chip that is still
fully programmable — retry it. Fuse writes are the only genuine brick, and the
datasheet notes SPIEN is "not accessible in serial programming mode", so ISP
cannot even disable itself. With the flash-only rule this cannot go
permanently wrong.

  avrdude -c usbasp-clone -p t841 -n                       # warm-up: the first
                                                           # ISP entry after an
                                                           # idle gap fails
  avrdude -c usbasp-clone -p t841 -U flash:w:<hex>:i

-c usbasp-clone, not usbasp: FASTUSBasp presents its own USB vendor/product
strings and avrdude's usbasp entry matches on those rather than VID:PID.

Expect: EEPROM wiped, the Pi power-cycled (setup() calls cutPower() first), and
the node returning by itself because 0 != 0x5A.

---
--- a/Firmware/WittyPi4/WittyPi4.ino
+++ b/Firmware/WittyPi4/WittyPi4.ino
@@ -65,7 +65,26 @@
  * readable/writable registers
  */
 #define I2C_CONF_ADDRESS            16  // I2C slave address: defaul=0x08
-#define I2C_CONF_DEFAULT_ON         17  // turn on RPi when power is connected: 1=yes, 0=no
+#define I2C_CONF_DEFAULT_ON         17  // turn on RPi: see DEFAULT_ON_BUTTON_VALUE
+
+// The ONLY value of register 17 that keeps the Pi off waiting for the button.
+// Every other value powers it on. Stock tested `== 1`, so ONE value of 256
+// powered on and 255 waited; on an unattended node that polarity is backwards.
+// The controller validates nothing -- no CRC, no magic, no range check -- and
+// the EEPROM load adopts any byte that is not 255 verbatim.
+//
+// 0x5A for DISTANCE: retention decay drifts toward the erased 0xFF, while an
+// interrupted write of the 0x01 this register normally holds can land on 0x00.
+// 0x5A is 5 bits from 0x01 and 4 from 0xFF, so neither mode reaches it.
+//
+// A wiped EEPROM now powers on by itself. i2cReg zero-initialises, and
+// 0 != 0x5A. That is why no seed is needed here -- which matters, because the
+// flash that installs this firmware erases the EEPROM (hfuse 0xdd, EESAVE
+// unprogrammed).
+//
+// Diverges from the vendor on ONE value: their documented "0 = no" now means
+// ON. Use 90 to keep a board off.
+#define DEFAULT_ON_BUTTON_VALUE     0x5A
 #define I2C_CONF_PULSE_INTERVAL     18  // pulse interval (in seconds, for LED and dummy load): default=4 (4 sec)
 #define I2C_CONF_LOW_VOLTAGE        19  // low voltage threshold (x10), 255=disabled
 #define I2C_CONF_BLINK_LED          20  // how long the white LED should stay on (in ms), 0 if white LED should not blink.
@@ -248,9 +267,22 @@
   sei();
 
   // power on or sleep
-  bool defaultOn = (i2cReg[I2C_CONF_DEFAULT_ON] == 1);
+  bool defaultOn = (i2cReg[I2C_CONF_DEFAULT_ON] != DEFAULT_ON_BUTTON_VALUE);
   if (defaultOn) {
-    delay(i2cReg[I2C_CONF_DEFAULT_ON_DELAY] * 1000);  // delay if the value is configured
+    // Masked, because this multiply overflows. The product is computed in a
+    // 16-bit int: above 32 it wraps, and 254 reaches delay() as 4,294,959,152 ms
+    // -- 49.7 days of rail-off, indistinguishable from dead hardware. That is
+    // not hypothetical; it cost a 23-minute outage on 2026-08-15.
+    //
+    // & 0x1F bounds it to 31 (31 * 1000 = 31000, inside int16) for 4 bytes,
+    // where a compare-and-clamp on load cost 12 and did not fit. Masking at the
+    // point of USE also protects the dangerous operation directly, with no
+    // branch to get wrong.
+    //
+    // The stored register is NOT changed, so a corrupt value above 32 still
+    // READS as itself while the delay behaves as value & 31. Only reachable via
+    // corruption -- the policy writes 10 and userspace refuses anything over 32.
+    delay((i2cReg[I2C_CONF_DEFAULT_ON_DELAY] & 0x1F) * 1000);
     updateRegister(I2C_ACTION_REASON, REASON_POWER_CONNECTED);
     powerOn();  // power on directly
   } else {
@@ -274,7 +306,10 @@
   i2cReg[I2C_CONF_PULSE_INTERVAL] = 4;
   i2cReg[I2C_CONF_LOW_VOLTAGE] = 255;
   i2cReg[I2C_CONF_BLINK_LED] = 100;
-  i2cReg[I2C_CONF_POWER_CUT_DELAY] = 70;
+  // 25.0 s, not the stock 7.0 s, and not the 20.0 s this patch first carried.
+  // Everything after a shutdown request shares this budget and it is now four
+  // terms: wake-guard + notification + an 8 s mark-good grace + a 6 s Linux
+  // shutdown = 20 s. At a 20.0 s seed that does not fit, so a freshly flashed
+  // board would run a budget its own software would refuse.
+  // 250 is WP_MAX_POWER_CUT_DELAY x10 — register 21 is deci-seconds in a byte
+  // and 255 is the never-written sentinel, so this is the ceiling.
+  // Free, as before: same instruction, different constant.
+  i2cReg[I2C_CONF_POWER_CUT_DELAY] = 250;
   i2cReg[I2C_CONF_RECOVERY_VOLTAGE] = 255;
 
   i2cReg[I2C_CONF_ADJ_VIN] = 20;
